<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Juan O'Donojú</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1762">1762</a>–<a href="/years/1821">1821</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Juan O'Donojú was a Spanish general of Irish descent and the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>, appointed by the liberal government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>. Arriving in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> in 1821 to find the country almost entirely in the hands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agustin-de-iturbide">Agustín de Iturbide</a>'s Army of the Three Guarantees, he signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-cordoba">Treaty of Córdoba</a> on 24 August 1821, accepting Mexican independence. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> a few weeks later.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/juan-odonoju.jpg" alt="Juan O'Donojú" width="320" height="409" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Juan_O%27Donoju.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
